WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.910
MICHAEL COLEMAN: Hi, I'm Mike
Coleman, a developer advocate

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:03.990
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.670
In this video, I
want to introduce

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.620
you to Migrate for Anthos.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.510
Migrate for Anthos
is a set of tools

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.810
that inspects existing workloads
running in virtual machines

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.420
and automatically creates
the needed container

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.730
artifacts for modernization.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.190
Let me break down that last
sentence into two parts

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:22.710
and talk separately about where
these virtual machines might

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:26.460
be running and what types of
artifacts Migrate for Anthos

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.050
creates.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.930
Migrate for Anthos can
migrate both Windows and Linux

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:31.710
applications.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:34.290
Windows applications can be
running on a Google Compute

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.430
Engine VM.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.380
In addition to Google
Compute Engine,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.290
Linux applications can
also be hosted on-prem

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:44.910
with VMware vSphere,
AWS EC2, and Azure VMs.

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.700
In Migrate for Anthos, where
your VM currently resides

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.710
is called the migration source.

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.710
For this video, the source
will be an application running

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.840
in a VM on Compute Engine.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.730
The output from
Migrate for Anthos

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.950
is a comprehensive
set of artifacts

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.780
that you can use to
deploy your newly

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.490
containerised application.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.620
These artifacts include
a Kubernetes YAML

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.590
file that you can use
to create Kubernetes

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.720
deployments and services,
a Dockerfile that

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.730
can be used to create
a new container image,

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.010
and other YAML
files that describe

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:16.020
what was actually migrated.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.587
Once you have these
artifacts, you

00:01:17.587 --> 00:01:19.920
can deploy the containerised
version of your application

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.160
to an Anthos cluster or to
the various flavors of Google

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.620
Kubernetes Engine,
including GKE running

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:29.175
on Google Cloud,
on-prem, or on AWS.

00:01:29.175 --> 00:01:32.370
So you have an application
running on a supported source.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:33.810
And you can use
Migrate for Anthos

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.530
to get it running
in a container.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.440
But why would you do that?

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.260
There are several key benefits
to migrating your workloads.

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:41.670
The first is density.

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.563
Containers are much
lighter weight than VMs,

00:01:43.563 --> 00:01:45.480
since they don't contain
the operating system,

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.910
and therefore, require
considerably fewer compute

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.440
and memory resources.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.270
This in turn allows
greater density

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.210
of workloads across your
clusters, fine-grained resource

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.450
allocations and overall
lower infrastructure costs.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.310
Another benefit is security.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.190
GKE offers automatic
operating system upgrades,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.710
freeing you from the burden
of maintaining your operating

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.750
system.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:10.009
You can also augment legacy
apps with modern services.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:13.040
GKE allows you to leverage
platform add-on services

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.380
to [? seemingly ?] integrate
up-to-date functionality

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:16.550
with existing apps.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:18.920
For instance, you can
take advantage of Istio

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.030
on GKE or ASM on Anthos to
automate network and security

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.460
policies, without changing
your application code.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.560
Additionally, you can
leverage Cloud Logging

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.080
and Cloud Monitoring
with your applications.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:33.230
Unified policy and integrated
resource management.

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:36.350
GKE allows you to focus on
managing applications, not

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.140
infrastructure.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.560
It offers the power of
declarative desired state

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.950
management with powerful
tagging strategies and selector

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:44.220
policies.

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:47.120
You can also move to modern
image-based management

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:48.320
and orchestration.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.720
Migrate for Anthos offers
a unique capability

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.360
to extract an application
from the VM workload

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.210
and construct a Docker
image and related artifacts,

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.000
which in turn, allows you
to modernize the application

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.580
lifecycle and
operations management.

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.640
This could include integrating
with a CI/CD pipeline,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.030
using tools like Cloud Build
to implement day 2 maintenance

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.020
procedures.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.210
Furthermore,
image-based management

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.790
enables customers to
use GKE to perform

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.850
rolling updates, dynamic
scaling, self-healing,

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:18.150
and more.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:21.110
So now you have a general idea
of what Migrate for Anthos

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.430
does and why you might
choose to use it,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.930
let's jump into an
actual migration.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:30.050
Migrate for Anthos has
three primary components--

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.060
the Discovery tool,
which assesses

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.660
how successful a migration of
a given application might be;

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.000
the Processing Cluster,
a Kubernetes cluster

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.980
that is used to
examine the source VM

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:42.530
and create the migration
plan, as well as execute

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:46.520
the actual migration; and
migctl, a command line tool,

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.030
like kubctl, for interacting
with the processing cluster.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.350
Migctl is already
installed in Cloud Shell.

00:03:53.350 --> 00:03:56.420
A migration generally consists
of the following steps.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.520
And don't worry if you're
not sure of what each

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.253
of these steps actually means.

00:04:00.253 --> 00:04:01.670
We're going to get
into that as we

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:03.920
move through this video series.

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.230
First, you need to
create a Google project,

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:09.800
set up service accounts,
and enable appropriate APIs.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.740
Next, you create a migration
processing cluster.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.620
Then you go into the process
of choosing and qualifying

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:17.690
your targeted workload.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.240
Once you've done that, you can
define your migration source,

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:23.480
using migctl to create
a migration plan.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.730
Then you adjust that
migration plan to ensure

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:27.860
that it meets your needs.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:29.960
Then you create the
deployment artifacts.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.390
And then you can deploy
your newly containerised

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:33.620
application.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:35.300
In the rest of this
video, I'm going

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:36.920
to talk about the
first two steps,

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.950
since they are largely the
same whether you are deploying

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:42.320
Linux or Windows applications.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.530
I will cover the remaining
steps in subsequent videos.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.940
Now that we have the
migration process laid out,

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.470
let's jump into setting up the
prerequisites for a migration.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.158
Migrate for Anthos requires
two service accounts.

00:04:54.158 --> 00:04:56.450
The first service account is
used by Migrate for Anthos

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.940
to access Container
Registry and Cloud Storage.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.440
You will use this
service account

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.750
when you install the Migrate
for Anthos components.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.780
And it requires storage
admin permissions.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.330
The second service account
enables Compute Engine

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.240
as a migration source and
it requires Computer Viewer

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.130
and Computer
Storage admin roles.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:14.930
I will actually create
this service account

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.730
in a follow-on
video, but I wanted

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:18.680
to make sure you
were aware of it.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.420
In addition to the
two service accounts,

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.190
you will enable
the following APIs

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:24.210
that are shown on the screen.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.030
The next prerequisite
we need to deal with

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:27.560
is the Processing Cluster.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.390
As mentioned before,
Processing Cluster

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.060
is a GKE cluster
running the actual code

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.030
that analyzes the source VM
and creates the deployment

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:36.050
artifacts.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.720
So I will create a one-node
cluster here in the console.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.950
The cluster itself is a
pretty standard cluster.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:42.840
We're going to name
it Processing Cluster.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.780
We're going to put
it in US-WEST2,

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.620
which is the same location as
my VM that I'm going to migrate.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.120
Under the default
pool, we're going

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.530
to change that to one-node.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:52.940
We only need a single node.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:56.660
And then for the actual nodes,
just a couple of things.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:01.370
Ubuntu image type and the N1
standard for machine type.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:05.240
So with those configurations,
we're ready to click Create.

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:07.470
And that will go off and
that will build our cluster.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.000
Now this is going to
take a couple of minutes.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:11.210
So we'll fast forward the
video and we'll check back

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.330
in when that's finished.

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:17.130
OK, it looks like the processing
cluster is ready to go.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.970
So the next step is
to actually install

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:21.993
the Migrate for
Anthos components.

00:06:21.993 --> 00:06:22.910
So we'll come up here.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:28.098
We'll click into Anthos,
Migrate To Containers.

00:06:28.098 --> 00:06:28.890
We'll come up here.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:30.930
And we're going to add
a processing cluster.

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.350
So click Add Processing Cluster.

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:35.960
And we're going to come
down to Linux workloads.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.390
And we're going to select that
processing cluster we just

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:39.840
created.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.500
Click Next.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:45.210
Then we're going to run a series
of commands in Cloud Shell.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.390
The first one is
going to be to enable

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:48.890
the APIs I mentioned before.

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:52.400
So we'll copy that,
paste that in,

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.940
and those APIs will get enabled.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:59.930
The next step is to go
ahead and create the service

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:02.450
account that's going to allow
us access to the Container

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:04.550
Registry and the storage--

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:05.270
Cloud Storage.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:10.122
So we'll paste that in and
create that service account.

00:07:10.122 --> 00:07:11.580
Then we're going
to go in and we're

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:13.500
going to assign
the storage admin

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.930
role to that service account.

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.640
So we'll paste this command in.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.472
That will create
the role binding.

00:07:20.472 --> 00:07:21.930
Then we're going
to go in and we're

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:24.090
going to download the
JSON key, so that we

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.970
have that to authenticate
to the necessary services

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:28.420
as we go forward.

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.180
And then finally,
let's clear the screen.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:31.770
It's getting a little crowded.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:33.240
I'm going to go
ahead and I'm going

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:37.920
to start the installation
process of actually creating

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:40.510
the Migrate for Anthos
components on the cluster.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:45.600
So using migctl, I do
a migctl setup install.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.960
And it goes through and it adds
all the necessary components

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:50.850
to perform the actual migration.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.800
This will take just a couple
of seconds to finish up.

00:07:53.800 --> 00:07:54.300
All right.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:57.690
So now I can run migctl
doctor and that'll tell me

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:01.080
the status of my installation.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:05.040
OK, let's check again
here with migctl.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.380
And migctl is reporting
back that the deployment

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.820
has completed successfully.

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:11.790
And we're now ready to perform
an actual migration, which

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:13.650
we're going to do in
our next two videos.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:15.180
We're going to have
one on migrating

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.340
Linux applications and
the other on migrating

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.200
Windows applications.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:21.210
To recap, in this
video, you learned

00:08:21.210 --> 00:08:23.280
the benefits and
features of Migrate

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.487
for Anthos, as well as how to
configure the prerequisites.

00:08:26.487 --> 00:08:28.320
Be sure to like and
subscribe, because we'll

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:30.810
be posting subsequent videos
in the series, including how

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.880
to migrate Linux and
Windows applications,

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.669
as well as best practices for
evaluating your applications

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.130
and what considerations you
need to be aware of when

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:39.960
planning a migration.

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:42.030
And to get started on
Migrate for Anthos today,

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:45.815
visit
cloud.google.com/migrate/anthos.

